  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 158 PM CST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nLooking to be a pretty cloudy day for the South Texas region, \nremaining mostly overcast through Thursday evening leading to \nanother cool day with clearing skies expected Friday morning. The \nmid-level trough responsible for the recent rainfall has moved \nfurther east, allowing for a small reprieve from the rain. Expect\nmostly cooler temperatures with highs in the mid to high 70's and\novernight lows dipping into the 50's for most of the CWA through \ntoday through Friday. With these temperatures, we can expect some \nareas of patchy fog to develop tomorrow morning near the Victoria \nCrossroads. Visibilities ranging between 1-4 miles, and 3-5 miles \nout in the Brush Country can generally be expected, but will \nultimately increase by the early afternoon as diurnal heating \nburns of any fog that forms.\n\nGuidance is suggesting a shortwave moving through our area late \nFriday night into Saturday morning as low-level onshore flow \nreturns. This will increase moisture advection, bringing PWAT values \nto around 1.50-1.7\", according to the GEFS Plumes, by Saturday. \nThere's a low to moderate (30-50%) chance for showers and \nthunderstorms for Saturday into Sunday morning, with slightly higher \nchances along the coast. Will keep monitoring as guidance updates\nthe closer we get to the weekend.",
